@echo off
:: ============================================================================
:: BRC B2 Upload credentials
:: ============================================================================
:: Copy this file to "b2-credentials.bat" in this same folder, then fill in
:: ONE of the two options below and the required settings under them.
::
:: b2-credentials.bat is read by BRC-B2UploadScriptGenerator.bat at generation
:: time (for the non-secret settings: bucket name, prefix, filters) and by
:: every *_B2Upload.bat it generates (at upload time, for the actual
:: credentials). Nothing from this file is ever written into a generated
:: .bat or .txt file - the generated files only ever contain %PLACEHOLDER%
:: tokens, which are filled in from THIS file's environment variables each
:: time they run.
::
:: SECURITY
:: - b2-credentials.bat is listed in .gitignore. Never commit your filled-in
::   copy, email it, or store it anywhere off this machine.
:: - Use a B2 Application Key SCOPED TO ONE BUCKET with read+write access -
::   never your master account key. Create one at:
::   B2 Console > Application Keys > Add a New Application Key.
:: - If this key is ever exposed, revoke it in the B2 Console immediately and
::   issue a new one.
:: ============================================================================


:: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
:: RECOMMENDED - Option A: a WinSCP saved site
::
:: No secret ever touches this file, any generated script, or any environment
:: variable read by anything other than WinSCP itself. Set it up once:
::
::   1. Open WinSCP > New Site.
::   2. File protocol: Amazon S3.
::   3. Host name: your bucket's S3-compatible endpoint, e.g.
::        s3.us-west-004.backblazeb2.com
::      (find yours on the bucket's page in the B2 Console, under
::      "S3 Compatible" details - it depends which region the bucket is in).
::   4. Access key ID / Secret access key: paste your B2 Application Key ID
::      and Application Key (see SECURITY above - scope it to this bucket).
::   5. Save the site under a name, e.g. brc-b2-archive. Tick "Save password"
::      if prompted - WinSCP encrypts it in its own configuration store.
::   6. Uncomment the line below and put that same name in it.
::
:: set "WINSCP_SITE=brc-b2-archive"
::
:: If both WINSCP_SITE and B2_KEY_ID (Option B) are set, WINSCP_SITE wins.


:: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
:: ALTERNATIVE - Option B: inline Application Key
::
:: Use this only if you can't set up a saved WinSCP site (e.g. a shared or
:: locked-down machine with no local WinSCP profile). These values sit in
:: THIS file in plain text - restrict its NTFS permissions to the account(s)
:: that need it, and see SECURITY above.
::
:: set "B2_KEY_ID=0123456789ab000000000001"
:: set "B2_APP_KEY=K001abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123"
:: set "B2_ENDPOINT=s3.us-west-004.backblazeb2.com"


:: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
:: REQUIRED with either option:

:: set "B2_BUCKET=your-bucket-name"


:: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
:: Optional:

:: set "B2_REMOTE_PREFIX=archive"
::   Folder inside the bucket to upload under. Leave commented out to upload
::   straight into the bucket root.

:: set "ONLY_STALE=1"
::   1 (default) = only rows where the CSV's IsStale column is True.
::   0 = upload every row in the CSV regardless of IsStale.

:: set "MIRROR_DELETE=0"
::   0 (default) = uploads only ever add or update files in the bucket.
::   1 = also DELETE remote files that no longer exist in the local folder.
::   This is destructive on the bucket side. Leave it off unless you
::   specifically want the bucket to mirror local deletions too.

:: set "WINSCP_EXE=C:\Program Files (x86)\WinSCP\WinSCP.com"
::   Only needed if winscp.com is not on PATH.
